          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Which alarm features in reflux does guidance say should change the plan?",
          "answer": "The guidance I could retrieve does not list the alarm features in reflux. The only reflux document returned, **GERD: empiric initial management and the alarm features that change it**, covers the starting approach for adults with typical heartburn and regurgitation and no alarm features. It describes a trial of acid-reducing treatment before any investigation. The passage cuts off before it names the alarm features, so I can't tell you which ones change the plan.\n\nThe search also returned a note from **Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis**. It says new, worsening, or rest-onset chest discomfort in someone with known heart disease needs urgent assessment. That note is not about reflux. It is relevant only because chest discomfort can feel like heartburn, so if you have chest pain, get it checked urgently rather than assuming it is reflux.\n\nTo find the alarm features, check the full version of the GERD guideline or ask a clinician, who can also judge your own situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
